WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.570
In 1989, Matt Groening launched The Simpsons.

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:07.990
Ten years later, he created another animated
comedy classic: Futurama.

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:13.110
The series centered around a late-20th-century
pizza delivery boy named Philip J. Fry who

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:19.609
gets stuck in a cryogenic freezing pod on
December 31, 1999, and wakes up in the strange,

00:00:19.609 --> 00:00:23.330
futuristic world of New New York in the year
3000.

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:26.970
Futurama had a devoted audience that stuck
with the show for over a decade.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:27.970
And now?

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:29.259
"Good news, everyone!"

00:00:29.259 --> 00:00:32.710
Here are some amazing stories you might not
have known about Futurama.

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:35.440
A different name

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:40.199
Futurama was, in retrospect, the perfect title
for a show about the amazing world of tomorrow.

00:00:40.199 --> 00:00:43.960
Still, creator Matt Groening had some other
names that he liked better.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.510
On the first season DVD director's commentary,
Groening says that his first ideas for the

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:51.750
show's name were Aloha, Mars, and Doomsville.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.270
But both titles were met with indifference.

00:00:54.270 --> 00:01:00.510
The title they eventually settled on was lifted
from the 1939-1940 New York World's Fair,

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:05.659
where visitors lined up for the General Motors
Pavilion and its exhibit called "Futurama."

00:01:05.659 --> 00:01:10.110
Fairgoers were welcomed to "the world of tomorrow"
— a glimpse into the far-off, futuristic

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:11.100
year of...1960.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.040
"Welcome to the world of tomorrow!"

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.619
"Why do you always have to say it that way?"

00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:20.920
"Haven't you heard of a little thing called
showmanship?"

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:21.939
Fry &amp; Homer

00:01:21.939 --> 00:01:26.299
On Matt Groening's first show, The Simpsons,
several of the main characters were named

00:01:26.299 --> 00:01:30.360
after Groening's immediate family — for
example, Homer and Marge Simpson got their

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.540
names from Groening's parents.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:36.770
Futurama's lead character, Philip J. Fry,
was also named after Groening's father — Philip

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:38.090
was his middle name.

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:42.710
This means that Homer Philip Groening, who
died in 1996, had the unique distinction of

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:46.170
lending his name to two major cartoon characters.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:47.899
The voice of Bender

00:01:47.899 --> 00:01:52.770
Bender Bending Rodriguez, the sociopathic,
alcohol-fueled bending robot, is probably

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:54.920
Futurama's most beloved character.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.320
"Bite my shiny, metal ass!"

00:01:57.320 --> 00:01:59.420
"It doesn't look so shiny to me."

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:01.259
"Shinier than yours, meatbag."

00:02:01.259 --> 00:02:06.399
Voice actor John DiMaggio discussed his inspiration
for Bender's voice on The Kevin Pollak Chat

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:07.100
Show:

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.840
"It was part drunk at the end of the bar.

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:13.160
It was part, uh, Slim Pickens.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:19.340
'What in the hell in the wide wide world in
sports is goin' on?!'"

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:24.060
"What in the wide, wide world of sports is
going on here?"

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:29.250
"Throw that in there and then you throw, there
was a guy my friend Ralph Calamino used to

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:33.000
do in college, and it was this Charlie the
Sausage Lover Guy."

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.430
"He loved sausages.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:36.150
We have all kinds of sausages.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:39.640
We have brat sausages, we have hot Italian
sausages, sweet Italian sausages, hot Italian

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:40.640
sausages."

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:41.640
"We have wursts!

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:42.640
All kinds of wursts.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:44.730
More and more sausages.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.170
It was ridiculous.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:50.810
You throw that all in a blender and that's
how you get Bender."

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:55.420
Interestingly, DiMaggio also said he auditioned
for the role of Professor Farnsworth with

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:56.680
the same voice:

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:58.260
"Good news, everyone!"

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.260
Of course, he didn't get the part.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:01.380
Probably for the best.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.410
Why not Zoidberg?

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:08.410
Voice actor Billy West pulled from two major
old-timey inspirations to create Doctor Zoidberg:

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:10.480
actor and host George Jessel...

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.270
"I certainly want to thank Bill for that warm
introduction.

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:17.840
I was listening to it for a minute and I thought
I was dead."

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:19.120
and actor Lou Jacobi…

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.120
"Why?

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:23.260
I just caught the 7:45 elevator down from
six!"

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:27.740
Both of those inspirations definitely give
a Jewish and Yiddish flavor to the character,

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:32.090
which West says he thought was appropriate,
given the traditionally Jewish "-berg" suffix

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:33.090
of the name.

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:37.920
"Zoid," meanwhile, is a futuristic, alien-sounding
word that has personal significance for Futurama

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:43.260
producer and writer David X. Cohen, who, while
in high school, created a computer game named

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:44.260
"Zoid."

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:48.730
He submitted it to Broderbund Software, which
not only rejected it, but spelled "Cohen"

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:53.600
wrong in the rejection letter — which seems
like something that could happen to Dr. Zoidberg.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:56.660
"Woot woot woot woot!"

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:58.320
Casting Phil Hartman

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:03.410
The late, great Phil Hartman specialized in
playing characters both obnoxious and oblivious,

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:05.350
such as Bill McNeal on NewsRadio.

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:10.710
He also voiced sleazy lawyer Lionel Hutz and
washed-up actor Troy McClure on Matt Groening's

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:12.350
other show, The Simpsons.

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:16.650
So it makes perfect sense that Hartman had
been cast to provide the voice of obnoxious,

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:20.150
Captain Kirk-esque spaceship captain Zapp
Brannigan on Futurama.

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:24.940
But after Hartman's tragic murder in 1998,
Billy West stepped into the role.

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:29.290
He'd once talked with Hartman over their shared
loved of old time radio announcers that had

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:32.500
big, booming voices, as well as a huge sense
of self-importance.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:37.960
So, West performed Zapp Brannigan in that
style as a tribute to Hartman, or at least

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:39.720
how he thought Hartman might've done the voice.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.470
"Why's it still blurry?"

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:43.550
"That's all the resolution we have.

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:46.090
Making it bigger doesn't make it clearer."

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:47.840
"It does on CSI: Miami."

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:49.330
"...ugh."

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:51.400
It's the Beastie Boys

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:54.920
Futurama's producers really wanted to line
up one of the most popular rap groups of the

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:59.380
20th century, the Beastie Boys, to play themselves
in the 31st century.

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:01.220
Too bad it never actually happened.

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:05.950
According to the DVD commentary for the episode
"Hell is Other Robots — in which Fry, Bender,

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:10.640
and Leela go see the heads of the Beastie
Boys in concert — writer/producers Ken Keeler

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:15.160
and David X. Cohen went to New York to meet
with the Beastie Boys, but never managed to

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:16.160
connect.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:19.030
Eventually, the band members were recorded
doing their vocals separately.

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:23.580
Well, Adam "Ad-Rock" Horovitz and Michael
"Mike D" Diamond recorded their tracks.

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:28.810
Adam "MCA" Yauch had a scheduling conflict,
so Adrock just did his lines for him.

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:33.610
Even after all that, the group wasn't entirely
happy with the material that Futurama writers

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:34.610
had prepared.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:38.910
In the finished episode, the Beastie Boys
perform a portion of their 1994 hit "Sabotage"

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:39.910
a cappella

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:42.520
"Oh my, it's a mirage."

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:48.640
"Tell you all, it's a sabotage!"

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:53.300
They'd initially been asked to do their 1987
hit "(You Gotta) Fight for Your Right (To

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:55.230
Party)" in this style, but refused.

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:59.130
Nevertheless, there were apparently no hard
feelings because the group gave a bit of a

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:03.270
shout-out to Futurama on their 2009 single
"Too Many Rappers."

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:07.090
One of the song's lyrics says that they'll
still be in the rap game "until the year 3000

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:08.680
and beyond."

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:11.280
The sad truth of Seymour

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.130
Futurama was always meant to be a comedy,
but some episodes would occasionally touch

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:20.040
on Fry's sadness and loss at being stuck in
the future, having left his life and loved

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:21.220
ones behind.

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:25.020
The 2002 episode "Jurassic Bark" is one of
those episodes.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:29.940
While visiting a museum, Fry discovers the
fossilized remains of his 20th century dog,

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:30.940
Seymour.

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:34.220
Professor Farnsworth tells Fry that he could
feasibly take some of Seymour's DNA and create

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:35.930
a clone.

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:39.880
During the cloning process, Fry learns that
Seymour lived to be 15.

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:44.639
He figures that after he'd disappeared in
1999, Seymour must have lived a long, happy

00:06:44.639 --> 00:06:45.960
life without him.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:50.560
And then, the saddest flashback ever broadcast
on television appeared: Seymour didn't move

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:55.270
on — he waited for Fry outside the pizzeria
where he used to work for more than a decade.

00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:59.690
The episode ends with Seymour closing his
eyes, passing away, and everyone watching

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:00.710
in tears.

00:07:00.710 --> 00:07:03.470
Amazingly, this episode could have been even
sadder.

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:08.010
As revealed in the episode's DVD commentary,
the writers' toyed around with Fry finding

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:12.639
the remains of his mother, and then considering,
but ultimately dismissing, cloning her.

00:07:12.639 --> 00:07:17.250
That didn't stop them from taking a tear-jerking
dive into Fry's mommy issues in another episode,

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:17.900
of course:

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:29.640
"I can't stand to see him like this.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:31.960
I'll push him off the roof."

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:33.160
Thanks for watching!

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:35.660
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:38.700
Plus, check out all this other cool stuff
